faq:
  - q: "What does cured in place pipe actually mean?"
    a: "It means the new pipe is manufactured inside the old one. A flexible tube soaked in liquid resin is placed in the damaged pipe and then hardened, or cured, right where it sits. The result is a rigid, jointless pipe that fits the inside of the host pipe exactly. Nothing is dug up and nothing is removed."
  - q: "How long does a cured in place pipe liner last?"
    a: "Specifications written by cities and state agencies design CIPP liners around a 50-year service life, and the first liner ever installed, in a London sewer in 1971, is still carrying flow. The liner is a thermoset plastic that does not rust, corrode or let roots in at joints, because it has no joints."
  - q: "Is cured in place pipe cheaper than replacing the sewer?"
    a: "On the real residential jobs posted on this site, lining came in roughly 30 to 50 percent below the dig-and-replace bid the homeowner already had in hand. The bigger the restoration bill would have been, such as a driveway, a slab floor or mature landscaping, the wider that gap gets. See the <a href='/cured-in-place-pipe/cost'>cost guide</a> for the full table."
  - q: "Does the liner make the pipe smaller?"
    a: "Slightly. A residential liner is typically a few millimeters thick, so a 4-inch drain loses a small fraction of its diameter. Because the new wall is smooth and has no offset joints or root masses, flow capacity usually ends up equal to or better than the cracked pipe it replaced."
  - q: "Can every sewer pipe be lined?"
    a: "No. A pipe that has collapsed, lost a section, or sags badly enough to hold standing water needs to be repaired or replaced at that spot before a liner can be considered. The camera inspection decides this. A good contractor will tell you plainly when lining is the wrong tool; the <a href='/cured-in-place-pipe/repair'>repair vs. replacement page</a> explains how to read that call."
  - q: "How do I know a contractor is qualified to line pipe?"
    a: "Ask for their contractor's license, the liner and resin brand they install, proof of manufacturer training on that system, and a before-and-after camera recording on every job. The <a href='/cured-in-place-pipe/training-and-certifications'>training and certifications page</a> lists what each of those should look like, and every installer on this site has been checked against them."
