<p class="cl-lead">Cured in place pipe, usually shortened to CIPP, is a way to build a brand-new pipe inside a broken one. A soft tube saturated with resin goes into the damaged line through an existing opening, gets pressed against the old pipe wall, and is hardened in place with hot water, steam or ultraviolet light. When the cure is done, the old pipe is a mold and the liner is the pipe.</p>

<p>For a property owner the appeal is simple: the sewer gets fixed and the yard, driveway and floors stay where they are. For a city engineer the appeal is the same thing at scale, a block of sewer main renewed overnight from two manholes. This guide covers how the method works, what it costs against digging, where it does and does not belong, the standards that govern it, and how to find a contractor who does it well. Each section links to a deeper page.</p>

<h2>What cured in place pipe is</h2>
<p>A CIPP liner has two parts. The <strong>tube</strong> is a flexible sleeve, usually needled polyester felt or a fiberglass fabric, with an impermeable coating on one side. The <strong>resin</strong> is a liquid thermoset, most often polyester or vinyl ester for sewers and epoxy for small-diameter drains and pressure lines, that soaks into the tube and later hardens permanently. Thermoset means that once it cures it cannot be melted back; it behaves like a rigid plastic pipe for the rest of its life.</p>
<p>The idea was first put to work in 1971, when British engineer Eric Wood lined an egg-shaped Victorian brick sewer in the London Borough of Hackney. That installation is still in service. Wood went on to found Insituform, and the inversion technique he patented is the one most residential crews still use. Today the method is written into the standard specifications of state transportation departments and city sewer utilities across the United States.</p>

<h2>How the lining process works</h2>
<p>Every <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe/installation">cured in place pipe installation</a> follows the same arc, whether it is a 40-foot kitchen drain or a 400-foot sewer main:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Camera and clean.</strong> A crawler or push camera records the pipe, then a hydro-jetter and cutting heads strip out roots, scale and debris so the liner can bond to clean wall.</li>
  <li><strong>Measure and wet out.</strong> The tube is cut to length and the resin is worked through it, by vacuum and rollers in the shop or on a truck-mounted table.</li>
  <li><strong>Install.</strong> The wet tube is either inverted into the pipe with water or air pressure (ASTM F1216) or pulled into place and inflated with a bladder (ASTM F1743).</li>
  <li><strong>Cure.</strong> Hot water, steam or a UV light train hardens the resin. Sensors track the temperature so the cure is complete end to end.</li>
  <li><strong>Reinstate and inspect.</strong> A robotic cutter reopens every branch connection the liner covered, and a final camera pass documents the finished pipe.</li>
</ol>
<p>On a residential lateral, all of that typically happens in a single day, often with the water back on the same evening. The installation page walks through each step with timing, what you will see and hear from the street, and the handful of things that can go wrong.</p>

<h2>What it costs compared with digging</h2>
<p>The honest answer is that <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe/cost">cured in place pipe cost</a> depends on length, diameter, how many branch connections need reopening and how much cleaning the pipe needs first. What makes the number meaningful is the alternative. The contractors on this site post the dig-and-replace bid the homeowner already had alongside what the lining job was actually done for. Across {{ savings.count }} posted jobs in {{ savings.states | join(", ") }}, lining averaged {{ savings.avgPct }} percent less than excavation, and the gap only widens when a slab, a driveway or a public sidewalk is in the way. The cost guide lays out every one of those jobs with footage and per-foot math.</p>

<h2>Where CIPP is used</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Residential sewer laterals</strong>, the 4- to 6-inch line from the house to the city main, usually clay, cast iron or Orangeburg. This is the bulk of the work the installers on this site do.</li>
  <li><strong>Under-slab drains and vertical stacks</strong> inside buildings, where opening the floor or walls is the expensive part.</li>
  <li><strong>Municipal sewer and storm mains</strong>, lined manhole to manhole, often several hundred feet at a time.</li>
  <li><strong>Commercial and industrial process lines</strong>, where downtime costs more than the pipe.</li>
  <li><strong>Pressure pipe</strong>, including some water and force mains, with reinforced liners designed for internal pressure rather than just soil load.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Lining, spot repair or replacement?</h2>
<p>A liner is the right answer when the host pipe is cracked, root-invaded, corroded or leaking at the joints but still holding its round shape and grade. It is the wrong answer when a section has collapsed, when a long run has sagged into a belly that holds water, or when the pipe is so undersized that a slightly smaller bore would create a new problem. Those pipes need a <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe/repair">cured in place pipe repair</a> plan that starts with excavating or bursting the bad section, and sometimes the whole run. The repair page explains how to read a camera report and judge which of the three you are looking at.</p>

<h2>Standards and testing</h2>
<p>CIPP is governed by a small set of ASTM standards that public owners write into their contracts: F1216 for inversion and heat cure, F1743 for pulled-in-place liners, F2019 for UV-cured glass-reinforced liners, D5813 for the liner material itself, and D790 for the bending tests that prove the cured liner is strong enough. The common minimums are a flexural strength of 4,500 psi and a flexural modulus of 250,000 psi, with wall thickness designed for a 50-year life that assumes the old pipe eventually contributes nothing. On public jobs a sample of the cured liner is cut and sent to a lab; the <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe/liner-testing">cured in place pipe liner testing</a> page explains what that report says and why a homeowner can ask for the same numbers. The site's <a href="/p/cipp-specifications">CIPP specifications page</a> collects the material details.</p>

<h2>The equipment behind it</h2>
<p>You can tell a dedicated lining crew from a plumber who dabbles by what rolls up to the curb. Inversion drums or shooters, a wet-out table or a refrigerated truck carrying pre-impregnated liner, a boiler or steam generator, a UV light train for glass liners, a robotic cutter and a crawler camera are the core of the kit. The <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe/equipment">cured in place pipe equipment</a> page describes each piece and what it tells you about the job.</p>

<h2>Finding a contractor</h2>
<p>Lining is a specialty, and the quality of the result depends almost entirely on the crew. Every installer listed on this site has been checked for licensing, work history and manufacturer training, and each one is listed for a defined territory. Start with <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe/near-me">cured in place pipe near me</a> for how to search by state, what to ask on the first call and what to do if your state has no listed installer yet, or go straight to the <a href="/all-contractors">full list of verified contractors</a>. If you want to know what credentials to look for first, the <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe/training-and-certifications">training and certifications</a> page is written for homeowners, not contractors.</p>

